  <h1>Training Journal: Turkey Trot 10 Miler and the Marathon Math</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">November 26, 2026 · Journal</div>

  <article>
    <p>Thanksgiving Day was my checkpoint, so here is the result: 10 miles in
    1:24:47, an 8:28 pace. I placed 69th of 173 overall and 7th of 12 in my age
    group (50 to 54).</p>

    <p>That is slower than last year. This is normally one of my best runs of
    the year, and I usually run it under 8:00 per mile. But I ran a half
    marathon five days earlier, so it is hard to know how to read it.</p>

    <h2>What went right and what did not</h2>
    <p>The good news is the right leg. It was the one that wrecked my last half
    marathon, and today it felt 100 percent. No pain at all.</p>
    <p>The less good news is that I was worn down overall by about mile 4 and
    spent the rest of the race managing it. That is early, and it tells me
    something about what the second half of a marathon would feel like.</p>

    <h2>What the calculator says</h2>
    <p>I put the 10 mile time through the same race equivalency math the
    calculator uses (Riegel, with the runner profile slider):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Textbook (1.06): 3:55:26, about 8:59 per mile</li>
      <li>Light on long runs (1.09): 4:02:20, about 9:15 per mile</li>
      <li>Fades over distance (1.10): 4:04:41, about 9:20 per mile</li>
    </ul>
    <p>My November half marathon (1:49:56) pointed to roughly the same place:
    3:56 to 4:04 depending on the profile. Two different races, similar answer,
    which is reassuring.</p>
    <p>My gut said 9:15 at best, and the math agrees with that. But "at best" is
    the key phrase. I have not run longer than 13.1 miles in this build, and my
    back is still tight. The formulas assume you trained for the distance, and
    I did not. So my plan is to treat 4:02 as the best case, not the target.
    I would rather start near 9:20 and 4:05, and let 9:15 happen late if I
    feel good.</p>

    <h2>So is this a positive?</h2>
    <p>I think yes. I raced 10 miles on tired legs five days after a half, on a
    leg that is healed, and still ran 8:28. The weak spot is the long end, not
    the legs.</p>
    <p>The December 12 marathon is still a training run for a marathon in
    January. I am leaning toward doing it, and the Black Friday entry discount
    is what makes it official. If I get it, I register.</p>

    <p>Here is the
    <a href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=even&amp;t=4:05:00&amp;eq=half&amp;eqt=1:49:56&amp;profile=1.090">4:05 finish plan</a>,
    about 9:20 per mile, if you want to see the splits.</p>
